</div>

<div class="row g-3 mb-4">
     <div class="col-lg-8">
          <div class="card h-100">
               <div class="card-body p-4">
                    <h2 class="h5 fw-semibold mb-3">What is TurboTables?</h2>
                    <p>
                         TurboTables is a small JavaScript library that turns a plain HTML table into a paged, sortable,
                         searchable and (optionally) filterable view of server-side data. It was built for line-of-business
                         web applications, where tables of records are the heart of most pages.
                    </p>
                    <p class="text-body-secondary">
                         It doesn't fetch or render data itself. You keep your own API and your own row templates
                         (Handlebars in this demo); TurboTables owns the controls and the state, and tells your code when
                         to load a different page, sort or filter.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="section-title mt-4">How it works</h3>
                    <ol class="steps list-unstyled mb-0">
                         <li>
                              <span class="step-number">1</span>
                              <div><span class="fw-semibold">Mark up your table.</span>
                                   <span class="text-body-secondary">Flag the headers as <code>sortable</code> or <code>filterable</code> and give the table its total row count.</span></div>
                         </li>
                         <li>
                              <span class="step-number">2</span>
                              <div><span class="fw-semibold">Attach TurboTables.</span>
                                   <span class="text-body-secondary">It adds the search box, sort icons, pager, page-size selector and loading overlay.</span></div>
                         </li>
                         <li>
                              <span class="step-number">3</span>
                              <div><span class="fw-semibold">Load the data.</span>
                                   <span class="text-body-secondary">When the user pages, sorts or filters, your data loader is called with the new state and a ready-made query string.</span></div>
                         </li>
                         <li>
                              <span class="step-number">4</span>
                              <div><span class="fw-semibold">Report back.</span>
                                   <span class="text-body-secondary">Call <code>endDataBinding(totalItems)</code> and the pager, counts and overlay update.</span></div>
                         </li>
                    </ol>
               </div>
          </div>
     </div>

